WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.640
Observability? Monitoring? Aren't they the same&nbsp;
thing? That's what a lot of people think. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.440
we're going to debunk that myth and five&nbsp;
others today in this video. Let's get to&nbsp;it.&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:19.920
Myth number one: Just a name. What do I mean&nbsp;
by that? I'm actually talking about how a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.760
people think that there's not much difference&nbsp;
between application performance monitoring and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.200
observability. But the truth is, it's they're&nbsp;
built for two completely different problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:37.760
sets. Application performance monitoring or APM&nbsp;
was built around the concept of seeing inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:48.640
runtimes. Now things like Java or .Net. But the&nbsp;
reason it works for APM is because in the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:56.360
runtimes, especially monolithic runtimes, all your&nbsp;
backend systems are tied to that same runtime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:06.400
And all your front end requests are coming in to&nbsp;
that same runtime. So if you have visibility here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.560
then you can see everything that's going on&nbsp;
in your system. But observability is built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.880
for modern applications that are built on top&nbsp;
of microservices environments with much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.400
complex systems. And while there are some&nbsp;
runtimes that might be in those systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.760
that's not enough for you to understand&nbsp;
exactly what's going on throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.600
entire microservice application. So the only&nbsp;
way to really do that is with observability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:38.480
which has the ability to monitor all parts of the&nbsp;
system--even going back to your backend systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.920
like your mainframe, so that you see a full&nbsp;
picture of everything that's going on in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:51.080
of your applications. Well, that was myth number&nbsp;
one. Now, let's take a look at myth number two:&nbsp;

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:58.360
Log love. What's log love? Log love is actually&nbsp;
referring to something I found out a few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:04.080
ago from an analyst who I was talking to, and he&nbsp;
asked me if I had heard about this situation where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:12.080
people were taking their metrics, traces and logs,&nbsp;
which everyone thinks of as observability and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.320
doing any monitoring, but rather just writing&nbsp;
all that information into log files. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:24.560
a problem occurred, going to the logs to solve the&nbsp;
problem. This isn't just a myth, this is an actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.240
anti-pattern. And anti-pattern is something that&nbsp;
seems like it's a good idea, but it's actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.880
the exact opposite result. And in this case, the&nbsp;
exact opposite result can be destructive to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:42.040
environment, to your applications, to your&nbsp;
business. Let me explain why. If you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:52.840
monitoring, that is, looking at all the different&nbsp;
pieces of your environment, plus seeing how your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.840 --> 00:03:01.560
end users are being affected by monitoring their&nbsp;
performance as well, and doing this in real time as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:07.720
it's happening. Then when you find out there's&nbsp;
a problem, say, through a trouble ticket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:13.960
that means that you're already too late to help&nbsp;
yourself. But by monitoring, you have the ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:20.520
to catch things before they happen. And the other&nbsp;
nice thing is by tying all the monitoring pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:29.120
together with your log information that you do&nbsp;
have, you actually speed up your troubleshooting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.680
So not only do you have the chance to get in front&nbsp;
of incidents before they impact your users, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:41.040
when an incident does occur, you have the ability&nbsp;
to solve it much faster. So when I think of M/T/L&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:47.360
in the world of monitoring versus logging, I just&nbsp;
like to put a little "2" on the M for metrics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:55.480
monitoring, traces and logs. Now that we've looked&nbsp;
at two myths, let's look at myth number three:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:02.400
Sticker shock. What are we talking about? You&nbsp;
probably want to hear about pricing and cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.600
for observability tools. And it's something that&nbsp;
we should look at because the reality is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:15.200
observability tools can be expensive, but they&nbsp;
don't have to be expensive. Let me explain why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.800
There is one way of pricing where everything, all&nbsp;
the features that you get and all the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:27.800
you do are inclusive and forecastable and known,&nbsp;
such as charging per host in your environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:34.560
This allows you to have a very steady price&nbsp;
quarter by quarter, year by year, based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.440
number of things that you're actually monitoring.&nbsp;
But there is another way that some observability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:45.000
solutions and monitoring solutions charge, and&nbsp;
that is to charge you for other things around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.040
system, such as the number of applications that&nbsp;
you're running or maybe the number of users that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:58.280
you have using your product, that's actually&nbsp;
using the observability tool itself. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:03.720
they're just looking at the amount of data that&nbsp;
you're sending through the system. They might even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:11.240
charge you just for debugging. And what happens&nbsp;
when this comes in is you don't know what's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:17.480
to happen ahead of time. So you're going along at&nbsp;
a fair clip. And then one or more of these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:23.720
happen and you end up with a quarterly surprise&nbsp;
charge. How big is that surprise charge going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:29.880
be? There's evidence out in the marketplace that&nbsp;
it could be $50 million or more. So when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:35.160
looking at your observability solutions, you&nbsp;
have to keep this myth in mind, not because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:39.080
definitely going to be there, but because there's&nbsp;
ways around it. But you want to be looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:44.760
solutions that have all inclusive pricing and have&nbsp;
a fair and forecastable way of giving you that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:53.660
price. Okay. We're halfway through the myths. Now&nbsp;
we're about to talk about myth number four: Who, me?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:59.120
That sounds really weird. Let's talk about&nbsp;
it. A lot of people think that observability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:06.280
is built to be used only by site reliability&nbsp;
engineers or SREs. But the situation with modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:12.040
applications-- and this is actually something that&nbsp;
goes beyond traditional monitoring capabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:19.720
--is that observability allows us to give the&nbsp;
information from the systems to the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:27.600
people and organizations that need it. So, for&nbsp;
example, you could get end user information to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:34.760
your marketing team. You can get performance&nbsp;
of different runtimes to your development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:43.560
organization. You can look at the system as a&nbsp;
whole sent to your DevOps team. Or, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:51.800
your SRE team and other IT personnel that need&nbsp;
to see what's going on. You can even include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:57.680
your business users and give them the information&nbsp;
they need. The fact is that observability takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:05.240
all the data that in traditional monitoring&nbsp;
is put just through your Ops power users and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:09.960
makes it democratized, giving everyone a view&nbsp;
of the data that they need so that they can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:17.640
their job as an application stakeholder.&nbsp;
Now, we've made it to myth number five:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:23.640
Pick favorites. Pick favorites? What do you mean&nbsp;
by that, Chris? Well, we've talked about the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:30.040
that we have all kinds of ways of pricing things&nbsp;
and observability tools. But one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:36.680
that happens is that it takes a lot of effort&nbsp;
to get traditional monitoring tools working. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:47.400
while a lot of organizations have anywhere from 8&nbsp;
to maybe 20 or even hundreds of applications, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:54.520
truth is that traditional monitoring tools require&nbsp;
way too much effort and work and cost to give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.520 --> 00:08:00.320
all this information. So you usually have to draw&nbsp;
a line and pick your favorite applications that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:09.040
you're going to monitor and the rest don't get any&nbsp;
monitoring at all. Why do I not like this? I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:13.880
like this because if you have an application,&nbsp;
it's important to somebody. Or as I like to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:18.280
every application is important to somebody. And&nbsp;
that means that there are stakeholders of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:23.640
applications, including business owners&nbsp;
and application owners and developers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:28.000
that need the information that comes from&nbsp;
observability. You shouldn't have to pick. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:34.880
that's why observability gives you this broader,&nbsp;
better view of the entire system, as opposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:42.120
making you pick just a few applications "just in&nbsp;
case". Okay, we're at the last myth. Myth number&nbsp;six:&nbsp;

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:51.880
DIY. The truth of the matter is you can&nbsp;
build monitoring yourself, but you shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:58.880
build monitoring yourself. Let me explain why.&nbsp;
When you add monitoring to all the pieces you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:08.520
have to do. Plus, when you add the idea of being&nbsp;
able to measure everything to the front end. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:15.400
and don't forget, you need to detect changes like&nbsp;
when a service disappears. Or maybe when a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:22.880
service appears within the system. Doing all that&nbsp;
manually requires you to slow things down. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:27.720
you're trying to accelerate your development, as&nbsp;
you're trying to get better as an IT organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:32.880
have better performing applications-- slowing&nbsp;
things down is a bad idea. In fact, it leads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.880
to lower quality applications. You want to speed&nbsp;
things up, and the only way to speed things up is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:44.040
to automate. And that's why you want to look for&nbsp;
an observability solution that automates things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:51.680
that automates discovery, that automates mapping&nbsp;
the system, that automates monitoring end users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:57.880
and does that for all of the different users&nbsp;
that you're going to bring into place across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:03.880
all the different applications that you have&nbsp;
to monitor and can see fully across the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:08.480
system and trace everything it needs to trace.&nbsp;
It needs to do this automatically, or else you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:14.240
going to slow down your development systems and&nbsp;
ultimately result in lower quality applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:20.960
and unhappy customers. And that's not what we&nbsp;
want. So look for automation and stay away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:29.040
manual observability. Thanks for watching. Before&nbsp;
you leave, make sure you hit like and subscribe.

